WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.860
After a nerve injury, a light touch can make a rat pull away.

2
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.280
What changed in the spinal cord, and who changed it?

3
00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:15.343
Meet a lamina I neuron, in the outermost layer of the dorsal horn.

4
00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:17.783
It sends signals toward the brain.

5
00:00:18.783 --> 00:00:21.883
In a simple picture, a light touch excites it a little.

6
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:25.143
Inhibitory synapses hold it below threshold.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:29.117
Those synapses open channels that pass chloride.

8
00:00:29.957 --> 00:00:32.837
They pull the voltage toward the anion reversal potential.

9
00:00:33.917 --> 00:00:39.917
That potential stays low because a transporter, KCC2, carries potassium and chloride

10
00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:40.917
out of the cell.

11
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:46.647
In 2003, Coull, De Koninck and colleagues studied rats after nerve injury.

12
00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:50.227
Their lamina I neurons had less KCC2.

13
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:53.987
Their anion reversal potential had shifted up.

14
00:00:54.727 --> 00:00:57.027
That does not mean the neuron itself depolarized.

15
00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:04.010
Predict. As that potential rises toward threshold, what happens to the inhibitory

16
00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.010
synapse's grip?

17
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.520
Recall the balance-point rule.

18
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.940
The synapse can only pull the voltage toward its own reversal potential.

19
00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.050
So as that potential rises, the pull weakens.

20
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.090
Less excitation is needed to fire the cell.

21
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:30.210
In a passive model with made-up values, this touch leaves the steady voltage below

22
00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.850
threshold in the healthy cell.

23
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:38.090
With the reversal potential at minus −55 mV, it sits exactly at the threshold

24
00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.930
boundary. At minus −50 mV, it sits above it.

25
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:44.553
Notice what did not happen.

26
00:01:45.473 --> 00:01:47.993
Inhibition did not have to turn into excitation.

27
00:01:48.913 --> 00:01:49.913
It only had to weaken.

28
00:01:50.983 --> 00:01:52.363
So what changed the neuron?

29
00:01:53.323 --> 00:01:54.323
Not a neuron.

30
00:01:54.933 --> 00:02:00.373
Tsuda and colleagues, in rats, found P2X4 receptors on reactive microglia.

31
00:02:01.293 --> 00:02:04.073
Microglia are the resident immune cells of the cord.

32
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:06.467
They ran two tests.

33
00:02:07.447 --> 00:02:09.907
Lowering P2X4 suppressed the allodynia.

34
00:02:10.707 --> 00:02:11.987
So the receptor was needed.

35
00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:17.797
Microglia stimulated with ATP were transferred into rats never injured.

36
00:02:18.697 --> 00:02:20.317
Those rats developed allodynia.

37
00:02:21.137 --> 00:02:23.037
So stimulated microglia were enough.

38
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.100
The messenger came in 2005.

39
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.160
Coull and colleagues showed that stimulated microglia release BDNF.

40
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:35.013
BDNF acts through TrkB receptors.

41
00:02:35.973 --> 00:02:40.393
It shifts the anion reversal potential up, the same change seen after injury.

42
00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:45.153
Block that signalling, and both the shift and the allodynia reverse.

43
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:48.667
Three papers, different links, all in rats.

44
00:02:49.527 --> 00:02:52.567
The behavioural readout was always a withdrawal threshold.

45
00:02:53.447 --> 00:02:57.287
The last link, from weaker inhibition to a touch that fires, is a model.

46
00:02:58.317 --> 00:03:03.877
Now the twist. Sorge, Mapplebeck and colleagues gave male and female mice a nerve

47
00:03:03.877 --> 00:03:07.037
injury. The hypersensitivity was comparable.

48
00:03:08.117 --> 00:03:12.257
In males, a microglial inhibitor reversed the hypersensitivity.

49
00:03:13.317 --> 00:03:16.237
In females, under the conditions tested, it did not.

50
00:03:17.217 --> 00:03:22.197
Predict. Intact females seemed to rely on adaptive immune cells.

51
00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:25.857
What does the inhibitor do in females lacking those cells?

52
00:03:31.917 --> 00:03:34.477
It works. The route can switch.

53
00:03:35.517 --> 00:03:38.217
A later study found the same pattern in rats.

54
00:03:39.057 --> 00:03:41.817
So it is not a quirk of mice, but it stays conditional.

55
00:03:42.867 --> 00:03:46.287
Macrophages in the dorsal root ganglion add another route.

56
00:03:47.187 --> 00:03:51.867
In mice, depleting macrophages throughout the body reduced hypersensitivity in both

57
00:03:51.867 --> 00:03:57.547
sexes. The authors favoured the ganglion macrophages, but skin dendritic cells were

58
00:03:57.547 --> 00:03:58.547
depleted too.

59
00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:03.710
So ask of any immune result: which cell, in which sex, in which compartment?
